# yes-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`yes-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `yes` command.
It outputs a string (default: `y`) followed by a newline, in an infinite loop, to stdout — until killed by a signal or a broken pipe closes stdout (treated as exit status `0`).

## Binary Name

The compiled binary must always be named **`yes-toto`**. Never rename it.

## Scope — Keep It Minimal

Supporting behaviour:

- Built-in `--help` and `--version` (detected anywhere in `argv`; `--help` wins if both appear)
- GNU-compatible strings: tokens such as `--foo` or `-` are ordinary output, not errors

**Do not add**:

- Interactive or TUI modes
- stdin reading
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Any feature that would break GNU `yes` behavioural compatibility

## Behavioural Contract

| Invocation | Output |
|---|---|
| `yes-toto` | prints `y\n` forever |
| `yes-toto hello` | prints `hello\n` forever |
| `yes-toto a b c` | prints `a b c\n` forever (args joined with space) |
| `yes-toto ""` | prints `\n` forever |
| `yes-toto \| head -n5` | prints 5 lines; exits `0` on broken pipe |
| `yes-toto --version` | prints `yes-toto <version>\n`, exits `0` |
| `yes-toto --help` | prints usage on stdout, exits `0` |

## Implementation Notes

See `implementation.mdc` for C11, 64 KiB buffer policy, `SIGPIPE` handling, hot-loop restrictions, stderr format, and justified deviations (e.g. optional `malloc` for very long joined argv strings before the loop).
